---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DotPlot from '../components/DotPlot.astro';
import LineChart from '../components/LineChart.astro';
import ChainsTable from '../components/ChainsTable.astro';
import ProductsTable from '../components/ProductsTable.astro';
import WideTable from '../components/WideTable.astro';
import MiddleTable from '../components/MiddleTable.astro';
import { ladderRuns, progress, TIER_LABELS, type Experiment } from '../lib/data';
import { pct } from '../lib/format';

// Colour follows the run, in the order runs were added (at most four on one chart).
const runs = ladderRuns().slice(-4);
const colors = ['var(--series-2)', 'var(--series-1)', 'var(--series-3)', 'var(--series-4)'];
const colorOf = (e: Experiment) => colors[runs.indexOf(e)] ?? 'var(--muted)';
const latest = runs[runs.length - 1];
const ladder = progress.ladder;
const won = latest ? ladder.filter((t) => t.startsWith('T') && latest.groups[t]?.won).length : 0;
const tiersTotal = ladder.filter((t) => t.startsWith('T')).length;
const withCurves = runs.filter((e) => e.curve.length > 0);
const loopBodies = ['sum a..a+n-1', 'a^n', 'fib(a,a)', 'x->3x+1', 'acc=(acc<<1)^a'];
const loopKinds = ['accumulate', 'repeat', 'recurrence'];
const lengths = ['n15', 'n63', 'n255'];
const cell = (e: Experiment | undefined, name: string) => e?.families[name]?.exact ?? null;
---

<Layout
  title="Results: in-context arithmetic at 16 and 32 bits"
  description="How well Teenie infers unseen integer functions at 16 and 32 bits, against lookup baselines: multi-step chains, products and calibrated confidence."
  keywords={['benchmark', 'lookup baseline', 'calibrated confidence', 'out-of-distribution generalization', '32-bit arithmetic']}
  kind="article"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Results</p>
  <h1>Where the model stands</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything here is measured at 16 or 32 bits. A lucky guess comes up once in 65,536 or once in four billion
    tries, and 999 examples cover almost none of the possible inputs, so a right answer has to come from working out
    the rule. Each score sits next to lookup methods that understand nothing, and next to how often the model was
    right when it was sure.
  </p>

  <h2>Rules with no textbook solution</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    37 held-out rules at 32 bits, each with a definite rule but no obvious way to work it out from examples. The score
    is <strong>bits recovered</strong>, 32 + log<sub>2</sub> of the probability the model gives the true answer: 0 is a
    blind guess and 32 is certain and right, while 10 means the true answer is about 1,000 times likelier than by
    chance. For the fitted baselines we trained gradient-boosted trees (one per answer bit) and a small neural network
    on each task's own 999 examples and calibrated them on held-back ones. Teenie reads the examples once and never
    trained on any of these rules. Green means ahead of every baseline by more than half a bit, red means behind; click
    a group for its rules.
  </p>
  <MiddleTable />
  <p class="small muted">
    Teenie wins wherever the rule needs real computation, like carries, products, automaton steps or a substitution
    table, and only narrowly on random expressions. It loses on rotations, which it never trained on and which a
    fitted tree can read straight off the input bits. Hashes and long mixing score about zero for everyone, as they
    should. We haven't compared against TabPFN yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Earlier model: unseen relations against lookup</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    The width-free model after its 24- and 32-bit stage, before any product training. Lookup is the best of five
    methods (the closest example, or matching one or two neighbouring bits), picked per relation after the fact.
  </p>
  <WideTable />

  <h2>Multi-step chains</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    The current model. A start followed by one or two of 21 kinds of step, with fresh random constants each time;
    beyond lookup counts only the questions every lookup method gets wrong.
  </p>
  <ChainsTable />

  <h2>Learning to compute through a product</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Held-out product chains at 16 bits as training went on. Matching examples can't get past about bit 5 of a product,
    and scratch registers were the first change that let the model compute through one.
  </p>
  <ProductsTable />
  <p class="small muted">
    At 32 bits the current model gets a*b right 89% of the time and one-step chains through a product 11%; what it
    still can't do is multiply a value it computed first, like (a+b)*a.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ladder">Earlier: the difficulty ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Before the move to wide numbers we scored every run on a ladder of test tiers (random expressions of 2, 3–4 and
    5–7 operations with held-out combinations, new operation types, loops), mostly at 8 bits. At that width the
    examples cover 1.5% of all inputs and lookup alone gets about a third of the sealed set, so these scores overstate
    what the model inferred; we keep them as a record. Each run is scored at the width in its title (8 bits unless
    stated). A tier is <b>won</b> at ≥50% exact with its sure answers right ≥95% of the time.
  </p>

  {
    latest && (
      <div class="grid-3 stats">
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">Tiers won by the latest run</span>
          <span class="value">
            {won} of {tiersTotal}
          </span>
          <span class="note">{latest.title}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">Hidden compositions</span>
          <span class="value">{pct(latest.groups.hidden?.exact)}</span>
          <span class="note">never generated for training</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">Unseen loop bodies (T5a)</span>
          <span class="value">{pct(latest.groups.T5a?.exact)}</span>
          <span class="note">{`at the trained length; loops run longer than trained (T5b, a stretch): ${pct(latest.groups.T5b?.exact)}`}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    )
  }

  <h2>Scoreboard</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <DotPlot
      title="Exact match per tier"
      reference={0.5}
      methods={runs.map((e) => ({ name: e.title, color: colorOf(e) }))}
      rows={ladder.map((t) => ({
        name: t,
        values: runs.map((e) => e.groups[t]?.exact ?? null),
        note: TIER_LABELS[t],
      }))}
    />
    <details>
      <summary>Table view</summary>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>tier</th>
              {runs.map((e) => <th class="num">{e.title}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              ladder.map((t) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{TIER_LABELS[t]}</td>
                  {runs.map((e) => {
                    const g = e.groups[t];
                    return (
                      <td class="num">
                        <b>{pct(g?.exact)}</b> {g?.won ? '✓' : ''}
                        <span class="small muted"> sure-right {pct(g?.sure_accuracy)}</span>
                      </td>
                    );
                  })}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
  <div class="tiers small muted">
    <p>
      <b>T1–T3</b>: random expressions of 2, 3–4 and 5–7 operations that always contain a combination never shown in
      training (a xor of a product, a product of a difference, a min of a sum, a shift of a xor). <b>T4</b>: operation
      types it never trained on, such as division, modulo, rotations and conditionals. <b>T5a</b>: loop bodies never
      generated (repeat count from b, up to 15 as in training) and unseen bit loops. <b>T5b</b>: loops run 63 or 255
      times, longer than anything trained; we treat this as a stretch goal. <b>Product, then a step</b>: a*b+b, a*b+5, a*b−a, (a*b)^a.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    withCurves.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>During training</h2>
        <p>Mean exact match per tier on fixed validation episodes (128 questions per relation), every 500 updates.</p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          {ladder.filter((t) => withCurves.some((e) => e.curve.some((p) => typeof p[t] === 'number'))).map((t) => (
            <div class="card">
              <p class="eyebrow">{TIER_LABELS[t]}</p>
              <LineChart
                title={`${t} during training`}
                xLabel="training updates"
                height={180}
                reference={0.5}
                series={withCurves
                  .map((e) => ({
                    name: e.title,
                    color: colorOf(e),
                    points: e.curve.filter((p) => typeof p[t] === 'number').map((p) => ({ x: p.update, y: p[t]! })),
                  }))
                  .filter((series) => series.points.length > 0)}
              />
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </>
    )
  }

  {
    latest && (
      <>
        <h2>Loops in detail</h2>
        <p>
          Latest run, exact match. Training only ever used repeat counts up to 15; 63 and 255 are longer loops. Some loops
          have short closed forms (the sum is a·n + n(n−1)/2), and finding either a procedure or a shortcut counts.
        </p>
        <div class="card table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>loop</th>
                {lengths.map((l) => <th class="num">count ≤ {l.slice(1)}</th>)}
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {loopBodies.map((body) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{body}</code> <span class="small muted">hidden body</span>
                  </td>
                  {lengths.map((l) => (
                    <td class="num">{pct(cell(latest, `${body} ${l}`))}</td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
              {loopKinds.map((kind) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{kind}</code> <span class="small muted">random bodies</span>
                  </td>
                  {lengths.map((l) => (
                    <td class="num">{pct(cell(latest, `${kind} ${l}`))}</td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    )
  }

</Layout>

<style>
  .stats {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tiers p {
    max-width: none;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
